255:2.3. Rational cuspidal curves with one cusp. The classification of rational cuspidal curves is a challenging old problem, with some conjectures (like the Coolidge–
256-Nagata conjecture [4, 14]) remaining open for many decades. The classification of
257-curves with a unique critical point is far from being accomplished; the special case
258-when the unique singular point has only one Puiseux term (its link is a torus knot)
259-is complete [5], but even in this basic case, the proof is quite difficult.
260:To give some indication of the situation, consider two families of rational cuspidal
261:curves. The first one, written in projective coordinates on CP 2 as xd + y d−1 z = 0
262-for d > 1, the other one is (zy − x2 )d/2 − xy d−1 = 0 for d even and d > 1. These
263-are of degree d. Both families have a unique singular point, in the first case it is
264-of type (d − 1; d), in the second of type (d/2; 2d − 1). In both cases, the Milnor
265:number is (d − 1)(d − 2), so the curves are rational. An explicit parametrization
266-can be easily given as well.
267:There also exist more complicated examples. For instance, Orevkov [18] constructed rational cuspidal curves of degree φj having a single singular point of type
268-(φj−2 ; φj+2 ), where j is odd and j > 5. Here the φj are the Fibonacci numbers,
269:φ0 = 0, φ1 = 1, φj+2 = φj+1 + φj . As an example, there exists a rational cuspidal
270-curve of degree 13 with a single singular point of type (5; 34). Orevkov’s construction is inductive and by no means trivial. Another family found by Orevkov are
271:rational cuspidal curves of degree φ2j−1 − 1 having a single singular point of type
272-(φ2j−2 ; φ2j ), for j > 5, odd.
273:The main result of [5] is that apart of these four families of rational cuspidal
274-curves, there are only two sporadic curves with a unique singular point having one
275-Puiseux pair, one of degree 8, the other of degree 16.
