974-6.4. Some examples and statistics. We will now present some examples and
975-statistics, where we compare our new criterion with the semicontinuity of the spectrum as used in [5, Property (SSl )] and the Orevkov criterion [18, Corollary 2.2].
976:It will turn out that the semigroup distribution property is quite strong and closely
977-related to the semicontinuity of the spectrum, but they are not the same. There are
978:cases which pass one criterion and fail to another. Checking the semigroup property
979-is definitely a much faster task than comparing spectra; refer to [6, Section 3.6] for
980-more examples.
981:Example 6.11. Among the 1,920,593 cuspidal singular points with Milnor number
982-of the form (d − 1)(d − 2) for d ranging between 8 and 64, there are only 481
983:that pass the semigroup distribution criterion, that is Theorem 1.1. All of these
984-pass the Orevkov criterion M < 3d − 4. Of those 481, we compute that 475
985-satisfy the semicontinuity of the spectrum condition and 6 them fail the condition;
986-these are: (8; 28, 45), (12; 18, 49), (16; 56, 76, 85), (24; 36, 78, 91), (24; 84, 112, 125),
987-(36; 54, 114, 133).
988-Remark 6.12. The computations in Example 6.11 were made on a PC computer
989-during one afternoon. Applying the spectrum criteria for all these cases would take
990-much longer. The computations for degrees between 12 and 30 is approximately
991:15 times faster for semigroups; the difference seems to grow with the degree. The
992-reason is that even though the spectrum can be given explicitly from the characteristic sequence (see [24]), it is a set of fractional numbers and the algorithm is
993-complicated.
