3-PLANE ALGEBRAIC CURVES OF ARBITRARY GENUS VIA
4-HEEGAARD FLOER HOMOLOGY
5-MACIEJ BORODZIK, MATTHEW HEDDEN, AND CHARLES LIVINGSTON
6-Abstract. Suppose C is a singular curve in CP 2 and it is topologically an
7:embedded surface of genus g; such curves are called cuspidal. The singularities
8-of C are cones on knots Ki . We apply Heegaard Floer theory to ﬁnd new constraints on the sets of knots {Ki } that can arise as the links of singularities of
9:cuspidal curves. We combine algebro-geometric constraints with ours to solve
10-the existence problem for curves with genus one, d > 33, that possess exactly
11-one singularity which has exactly one Puiseux pair (p; q). The realized triples
12-(p, d, q) are expressed as successive even terms in the Fibonacci sequence.
13-
14-1. Singular curves
15-Let C be an algebraic curve in CP 2 , defined as the zero set of a homogeneous
16:polynomial f of degree d. Such a curve is called cuspidal if the singular points of
17:C are all unibranched; that is, the singular points are isolated and the link of each
18-singularity is a knot in S 3 (such knots are often called algebraic knots). Cuspidal
19-curves form a natural family of algebraic curves that are topologically embedded
20-surfaces.
21:The theory of cuspidal curves of higher genus has not drawn as much attention
22:as the case of rational cuspidal curves, those of topological genus zero. One of
23-the inherent difficulties in the higher genus setting is that the complement of a
24:curve is not a rational homology ball (in the language of algebraic geometry, a Q–
25-acyclic surface, see [10]). The effect of this is that one of the main tools in studying
26:rational cuspidal curves, namely the “semicontinuity of the spectrum,” which is a
27-main ingredient of a classification result in [9], becomes considerably less restrictive
28-if the genus is greater than zero. Section 10 presents a more detailed discussion of
29-these issues.
