152-1.3. Applications and perspectives. Apart from an interest of its own of Theorems 6.4
153-and 6.18, we provide some specific applications. First of all, there is a growing interest in the
154:question of “trading genus for double points”. To be more precise, given a surface of genus g,
155-one can ask whether it is possible to deform it to a genus g ´ 1 surface with an extra positive or
156:negative double point. In the context of the surfaces in a four-ball with fixed boundaries, this
157-question is related to studying the difference between the clasp number and the smooth four-ball
158-genus; see [9, 12, 23, 27, 37]. We deal with a variation of this question, which concerns trading
159:genus of a closed surface in CP 2 for double points, while preserving the remaining singularities.
160-Given that examples of higher genus (and higher degree) curves in CP 2 do not appear often
161-in the literature, it is much harder to provide concrete cases, where our obstructions can be
162-applied. We provide the following two concrete examples.
