   101	   Let C be a curve. A point z ∈ C is called singular if the gradient of F vanishes
   102	at z. Singular points of irreducible curves in CP 2 are always isolated. Given a
   103	singular point and a sufficiently small ball B ⊂ CP 2 around z, we call K = C ∩ ∂B
   104	the link of the singular point. The singular point is called cuspidal or unibranched
   105	if K is a knot, that is a link with one component, or equivalently, if there is an
   106	analytic map ψ from a disk in C onto C ∩ B.
