   113	details. A unibranched singular point is topologically equivalent to one for which the
   114	local parametrization ψ is given in local coordinates (x, y) on B by t 7→ (x(t), y(t)),
   115	where x(t) = tp , y(t) = tq1 + . . . + tqn for some positive integers p, q1 , . . . , qn
   116	satisfying p < q1 < q2 < . . . < qn . Furthermore, if we set Di = gcd(p, q1 , . . . , qi ),
   117	then Di does not divide qi+1 and Dn = 1. The sequence (p; q1 , . . . , qn ) is called
   118	the characteristic sequence of the singular point and p is called the multiplicity.
   119	Sometimes n is referred to as the number of Puiseux pairs, a notion which comes
   120	from an alternative way of encoding the sequence (p; q1 , . . . , qn ). We will say that
