   807	6.4. Some examples and statistics. We will now present some examples and
   808	statistics, where we compare our new criterion with the semicontinuity of the spec-
   809	trum as used in [5, Property (SSl )] and the Orevkov criterion [18, Corollary 2.2].
   810	It will turn out that the semigroup distribution property is quite strong and closely
   811	related to the semicontinuity of the spectrum, but they are not the same. There are
   812	cases which pass one criterion and fail to another. Checking the semigroup property
   813	is definitely a much faster task than comparing spectra; refer to [6, Section 3.6] for
   814	more examples.
   815	Example 6.11. Among the 1,920,593 cuspidal singular points with Milnor number
   816	of the form (d − 1)(d − 2) for d ranging between 8 and 64, there are only 481
   817	that pass the semigroup distribution criterion, that is Theorem 1.1. All of these
   818	pass the Orevkov criterion M < 3d − 4. Of those 481, we compute that 475
   819	satisfy the semicontinuity of the spectrum condition and 6 them fail the condition;
   820	these are: (8; 28, 45), (12; 18, 49), (16; 56, 76, 85), (24; 36, 78, 91), (24; 84, 112, 125),
   821	(36; 54, 114, 133).
   822	Remark 6.12. The computations in Example 6.11 were made on a PC computer
   823	during one afternoon. Applying the spectrum criteria for all these cases would take
   824	much longer. The computations for degrees between 12 and 30 is approximately
   825	15 times faster for semigroups; the difference seems to grow with the degree. The
   826	reason is that even though the spectrum can be given explicitly from the charac-
   827	teristic sequence (see [24]), it is a set of fractional numbers and the algorithm is
   828	complicated.
