    23	                                                                            1. Singular curves
    24	                                             Let C be an algebraic curve in CP 2 , defined as the zero set of a homogeneous
    25	                                         polynomial f of degree d. Such a curve is called cuspidal if the singular points of
    26	                                         C are all unibranched; that is, the singular points are isolated and the link of each
    27	                                         singularity is a knot in S 3 (such knots are often called algebraic knots). Cuspidal
    28	                                         curves form a natural family of algebraic curves that are topologically embedded
    29	                                         surfaces.
    30	                                             The theory of cuspidal curves of higher genus has not drawn as much attention
    31	                                         as the case of rational cuspidal curves, those of topological genus zero. One of
    32	                                         the inherent difficulties in the higher genus setting is that the complement of a
    33	                                         curve is not a rational homology ball (in the language of algebraic geometry, a Q–
    34	                                         acyclic surface, see [10]). The effect of this is that one of the main tools in studying
    35	                                         rational cuspidal curves, namely the “semicontinuity of the spectrum,” which is a
    36	                                         main ingredient of a classification result in [9], becomes considerably less restrictive
    37	                                         if the genus is greater than zero. Section 10 presents a more detailed discussion of
    38	                                         these issues.
    39	                                             The goal of this paper is to investigate the singularities of cuspidal curves. To
    40	                                         state the main results, we need some background. To each singular point one
    41	                                         associates the δ–invariant and the Milnor number µ, which for unibranched singular
    42	                                         points are related by µ = 2δ. By definition, δ is the 3–genus of the associated linking
    43	                                         circle K. The genus g of C and the δ–invariants, {δi }, of the set of singular points,
    44	                                         {zi }ni=1 , are related by the genus formula:
