    59	   Our goal is to find constraints on the possible sets of singularities beyond those
    60	given by the genus formula. The basic idea is topological. We suppose that a curve
    61	C ⊂ CP 2 with some collection of singularities exists. Let Y (C) denote the three-
    62	manifold which arises as the boundary of a closed regular neighborhood N (C) of
    63	C. The homeomorphism type of this three-manifold depends only on the degree,
    64	genus, and the links of the singularities of C. The complement CP 2 −Int(N (C)) is a
    65	smooth four-manifold with particularly simple algebraic topology; in particular, its
    66	intersection form is identically zero. To show that C cannot exist, then, it suffices
    67	to show that Y (C) cannot bound a four-manifold with trivial intersection form.
    68	For this, we use invariants derived from the Heegaard Floer homology of Y (C) [28].
    69	These invariants are a generalization of the influential “correction terms” associated
    70	to rational homology three-spheres used in the study of homology cobordism groups
    71	and knot concordance. Among the many important references, we mention just
    72	one, [12], which informed our original work on this paper.
    73	   In the present situation, two particularly useful invariants derived from the Hee-
    74	gaard Floer homology complexes associated to Y (C) present themselves, which we
    75	refer to as the “bottom” and “top” correction terms. They depend on a choice of
    76	Spinc structure whose Chern class is torsion. The key feature of these invariants is
    77	that their values bound the characteristic numbers of smooth negative semi-definite
    78	four-manifolds bounded by Y (C), where negative semi-definite means that the self-
    79	intersection of any closed surface is non-positive. Notice that CP 2 − Int(N (C)) is
    80	negative semi-definite with either of its orientations.
    81	   One must have means to compute the correction terms. As a first step, we show
    82	that Y (C) has a simple description as surgery on a knot in the connected sum of
    83	copies of S 1 × S 2 . With this, along with the fact that the links of the singularities
    84	are all L–space knots [13] (in particular, their knot Floer homology complexes are
    85	determined by their Alexander polynomials), the computation of the correction
    86	terms becomes algorithmic by way of a surgery formula [26].
