    14	                                                                                        1. Introduction
    15	                                                 Let C be an irreducible projective plane curve in the complex projective space P2 with singular
    16	                                             points {pi }νi=1 . It is a very interesting, and still open problem, to characterize the local embedded
    17	                                             topological types of local singular germs (C, pi ) ⊂ (P2 , pi ) which can be realized by such a projective
    18	                                             curve C of degree d. This remarkable problem is not only important for its own sake, but it is also
    19	                                             connected with crucial properties, problems and conjectures in the theory of open surfaces.
    20	                                                 For instance, the open surface P2 \ C is Q-acyclic if and only if C is a rational cuspidal curve. On
    21	                                             the other hand, the rigidity conjecture proposed by Flenner and Zaidenberg in [13] (and supported
    22	                                             by many examples, see [13], [14], [15], [9]) says that every Q-acyclic affine surfaces Y with logarithmic
    23	                                             Kodaira dimension κ̄(Y ) = 2 must be rigid. (E.g., if C has at least three cusps then κ̄(P2 \ C) = 2,
    24	                                             cf. [47].) This conjecture for Y = P2 \ C would imply the projective rigidity of the curve C in the
    25	                                             sense that every equisingular deformation of C in P2 would be projectively equivalent to C.
    26	                                                 Among other interesting related open problems we mention: every rational cuspidal curve can
    27	                                             be transformed by a Cremona transformation into a line (this is the Coolidge-Nagata problem, see
    28	                                             [6],[27]); or, the determination of the maximal number of cusps among all the rational cuspidal
    29	                                             plane curves (proposed by F. Sakai in [16]) – this number is expected to be small (the maximal
    30	                                             known by the authors is four). In a recent preprint by K. Tono [40] it is proved that the maximal
    31	                                             number is strictly less than nine. Finally, we also list the conjectured numerical inequality (2.3) [35]
    32	                                             of S.Yu. Orevkov. [In forthcoming articles we plan to run the machinery of the present manuscript
    33	                                             for these open problems as well – at least when κ̄(P2 \ C) < 2.]
    34	                                                 The first-mentioned ‘characterization problem’ (on the realization of prescribed topological types
    35	                                             of singularities) has a long and rich history providing many interesting compatibility properties con-
    36	                                             necting local invariants of the germs {(C, pi )}i with some global invariants of C (like its degree, or
    37	                                             the log-Kodaira dimension of P2 \ C, etc.). Most of the compatibility properties are mere identities
    38	                                             or inequalities (see e.g. the Matsuoka-Sakai inequality, or Orevkov’s sharp inequality, both being
    39	                                             consequences of the logarithmic version of the Bogomolov-Miyaoka-Yau inequality). The more com-
    40	                                             plex Varchenko’s compatibility property is provided by the semicontinuity of the spectral numbers
    41	                                             of isolated hypersurface singularities, and it consists of a finite set of inequalities.
    42	                                                 Our goal is to propose a new compatibility property – valid for rational cuspidal curves C – which
    43	                                             seems to be rather powerful (see section 3 for some comparison with some classical criterions). Its
    44	                                             formulation is surprisingly very elementary. Consider a collection (C, pi )νi=1 of locally irreducible
    45	                                             plane curve singularities (i.e. cusps), let ∆i (t)
    46	                                                                                              Qbe the characteristic polynomials of the monodromy
    47	                                             action associated with (C, pi ), and ∆(t) := i ∆i (t). Its degree is 2δ, where δ is the sum of the
    48	                                             delta-invariants of the singular points. Then ∆(t) can be written as 1 + (t − 1)δ + (t − 1)2 Q(t) for
    49	                                             some polynomial Q(t). Let cl be the coefficient of t(d−3−l)d in Q(t) for any l = 0, . . . , d − 3.
